On Nov 15, 2010, at 8:47 PM, Philip Leifeld wrote:

> Hi,
> 
> I have developed a Java application which does some computations and can then export numerical data to a CSV file. Now I would like to access the export class of this application directly from R. I am basically trying to get the data directly into R instead of doing this via a CSV file.
> 
> I suppose RJava can help me to achieve this goal. Unfortunately, I have encountered the following problem. I hope you can point me into the right direction.
> 
> In the Java class, the output is a two-dimensional array of type double[][]. I have managed to get one-dimensional arrays (i.e., double[]) from the Java application into R via the following command:
> 
> > result <- .jcall(exportClass, "[D", "exportMethod")
> 
> But I have no clue how to replace "[D" by the correct return type for two-dimensional arrays. I tried "[[D", but it returns something like:
> 
> [[1]]
> [1] "Java-Array-Object[D:[D@fd68b1"
> 
> [[2]]
> [1] "Java-Array-Object[D:[D@e45076"
> 
> I have also found the method .jevalArray(), but .jevalArray(result) returns an error which says that the "object is not a Java object reference (jobjRef/jarrayRef)". I don't know if this is the right method anyway.
> 

It is the right method for the *elements*, so you simply want

.jevalArray(result[[1]])

(or use lapply for a more generic case)

If you have larger arrays it's usually faster to create a one-dimensional array in Java instead since all arrays are vectors (=1d) by definition.

Cheers,
Simon


